ver-cap-abort and timeout paths don't double-consume the pipes.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.501,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["_proc.reap_process_group calls `proc.communicate(timeout=grace)` on both the SIGTERM-grace path (line 89) and the SIGKILL path (line 101)","Plan: 'Reuse rebar._proc.reap_process_group for timeout and output-cap abort cleanup' — cap-abort is a NEW caller not present today (current _terminate is only invoked on TimeoutExpired)","Plan item 1 replaces communicate() with incremental selectors reads and 'keep byte counters before appending chunks to the retained buffers', implying the pipes are read manually before any reap runs"],"finding":"The plan reuses rebar._proc.reap_process_group for both the timeout path and the NEW over-cap-abort path, but reap_process_group internally calls proc.communicate(); after the plan's selectors loop has incrementally read (and typically closed) stdout/stderr, a subsequent communicate() on already-consumed or closed pipes can raise ValueError/mis-read. The plan does not specify the handoff ordering (whether pipes stay open until reaped, or whether reap must tolerate already-EOF streams).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f93dd8270283dad9f","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Context items 1-2 and AC lines 'Timeout and cap cleanup delegates process-group reaping to rebar._proc.reap_process_group'","norm_id":"n3cac4cd6d9779dbf","priority":0.099,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Over-cap stdout/stderr abort: selectors loop has partially read and closed a pipe, then reap_process_group's communicate() runs against it","Timeout after partial incremental reads: same double-consume of pipe file objects"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committe